{"records":[{"id":"ybhm5zb346","title":"Structure and assembly of the Enterohemorrhagic Escherichia coli type 4 pilus ","description":"The data represent raw images of the EHEC pilus assembly assays described in Bardiaux et al. Strutucre 2019.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-03-11T17:19:22.674Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ybhm5zb346","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ybhm5zb346"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Olivera","last_name":"Francetic","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hh8dkmpch7","title":" Escherichia coli K12 under plantaricin BM-1 reveals multiple affected metabolic pathways and peptidoglycan synthesis","description":" Escherichia coli K12 under plantaricin BM-1 reveals multiple affected metabolic pathways and peptidoglycan synthesis","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-09-30T23:56:11.432Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hh8dkmpch7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hh8dkmpch7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Huan","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Natural Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"b2znmbxstc","title":"Antibacterial Activity and Membrane-Associated Effects of PAN-RAS-IN-1 against Multidrug-Resistant Extraintestinal Pathogenic Escherichia coli","description":"original dates of Antibacterial Activity and Membrane-Associated Effects of PAN-RAS-IN-1 against Multidrug-Resistant Extraintestinal Pathogenic Escherichia coli","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-25T19:07:51.741Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/b2znmbxstc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/b2znmbxstc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"wenjia","last_name":"lu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Medication"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hbmnzwkk93","title":"Phage-encoded small RNA hijacks host replication machinery to support the phage lytic cycle","description":"Bacteriophages (phages) are major drivers of bacterial population dynamics, yet the significance of post-transcriptional regulation during infection remains largely unexplored. Central to this regulatory layer are small RNAs (sRNAs), which regulate target mRNAs via base-pairing, typically facilitated by RNA chaperones such as Hfq. Here, we applied RNA interaction by ligation and sequencing (RIL-seq) to comprehensively map the in vivo RNA-RNA interaction network in Escherichia coli during phage lambda infection. This analysis revealed extensive reprogramming of E. coli-E. coli interactions, phage-specific lambda-lambda interactions, and interkingdom interactions between phage and host RNAs. Among these, we identified a phage-encoded sRNA, phage replication enhancer sRNA (PreS), embedded within the early left operon. PreS regulates essential host genes, including dnaN, which encodes the DNA polymerase β sliding clamp. This regulation enhances DNA replication and fine-tunes the phage lytic cycle. These findings uncover an RNA-level regulatory layer in phage-host interactions and demonstrate how a phage-encoded sRNA can hijack host replication machinery to optimize infection.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-01T15:52:46.605Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hbmnzwkk93","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hbmnzwkk93"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Aviezer","last_name":"Silverman","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Raneem","last_name":"Nashef","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Reut","last_name":"Wasserman","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tamar","last_name":"Noy","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Susan","last_name":"Born","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tianyou","last_name":"Yao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuncong","last_name":"Geng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hila","last_name":"Rotbard","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Adi","last_name":"Levkowitz","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yotam","last_name":"Kaufman","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ido ","last_name":"Golding","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sahar","last_name":"Melamed","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biologic Imaging"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"b32w8b488z","title":"Intra-individual Escherichia coli diversity in Australian pinniped pups","description":"This file details the Escherichia coli phylotypes and corresponding B2 sub-types for E. coli isolates collected from free-ranging Australian fur seal (Arctocephalus pusillus doriferus), Australian sea lion (Neophoca cinerea) and long-nosed fur seal (Arctocephalus forsteri). Faecal samples were collected from ten pups from each species and subsequently cultured on selective media. Ten isolates of E. coli were sub-cultured from the original culture and analysed using quadruplex phylotyping and multiplex sub-typing PCRs to assign isolates to E. coli phylotypes and sub-types. The relationship between host factors (species, sex, body weight [kg] and standard length [cm]) was also investigated. This study found low intra-individual variation, suggesting that a single isolate of E. coli can provide an idea of the general abundance of E. coli phylotypes in free-ranging pinniped populations, and can be used as an efficient and cost-effective tool for monitoring the presence and abundance of human-associated phylotypes of E. coli. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-07-25T18:26:25.322Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/b32w8b488z","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/b32w8b488z"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mariel","last_name":"Fulham","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rachael","last_name":"Gray","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michelle","last_name":"Power","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bridget","last_name":"Webster","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microbiology","Coastal Pollution","Wildlife","Marine Pollution"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vn3bvngtc2","title":"LC-MS/MS data of bioengineering modification of the glycome using Salmonella (Ying Sheng)","description":"We established a metabolic-engineered host cell model that generates controlled glycan structure phenotypes on the cell surface. Specifically,  we produced host cell surfaces that were primarily fucosylated, sialylated, undecorated complex type structures, and high mannose-type structures. The manipulated host cell N-glycomes were  characterized and confirmed by liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry (LC-MS/MS) \nanalysis. Furthermore, glycoproteomic analysis yielded site-specific N-glycosylation information. Glycopeptides were  identified and quantified using a standard glycoproteomic workflow. Collectively, our results suggested the importance of glycans in host-microbe interactions and provided a novel insight into the significance of host glycome during pathogenesis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-02-26T12:39:40.394Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vn3bvngtc2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vn3bvngtc2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ying","last_name":"Sheng","orcid":"0000-0002-6002-9783"},{"first_name":"Sheryl Joyce","last_name":"Grijaldo-Alvarez","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Maurice","last_name":"Wong","orcid":"0000-0003-3851-2928"},{"first_name":"Yixuan","last_name":"Xie","orcid":"0000-0002-8512-6053"},{"first_name":"Carlito","last_name":"Lebrilla","orcid":"0000-0001-7190-5323"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mass Spectrometry","Tandem Mass Spectrometry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9k8txjbw9s","title":"Systems biology approaches to elucidate mucin sugar metabolisms in Escherichia coli","description":"Metabolic flux balance analysis for Escherichia coli that metabolizes mucin sugars.\nFor calculating metabolisms of mucin sugars in Escherichia coli, the iML1515 genome-scale metabolic model is used (Monk, J., Lloyd, C., Brunk, E. et al. Nat Biotechnol 35, 904–908 (2017)). ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-12-16T12:29:00.202Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9k8txjbw9s","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9k8txjbw9s"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yujin","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jungyeon","last_name":"Kim","orcid":"0000-0003-1193-0919"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Metabolic Flux Analysis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6knr855zx5","title":"Emergence of ion-channel mediated electrical oscillations in Escherichia coli biofilms","description":"ABSTRACT\nBacterial biofilms are communities of bacteria usually attached to solid strata and often differentiated into complex structures. Communication across biofilms has been shown to involve chemical signaling and, more recently, electrical signaling in Gram positive biofilms. We report for the first time, community-level synchronized membrane potential dynamics in three-dimensional E. coli biofilms. Two hyperpolarization events are observed in response to\nlight stress. The first requires mechanically sensitive ion channels (MscK, MscL and MscS) and the second needs the Kch-potassium channel. The channels mediated both local spiking of single E. coli biofilms and long-range coordinated electrical signaling in E. coli biofilms. The electrical phenomena are explained using Hodgkin-Huxley and 3D fire-diffuse-fire agentbased models. These data demonstrate that electrical wavefronts based on potassium ions are a mechanism by which signaling occurs in Gram negative biofilms and as such may represent a conserved mechanism for communication across biofilms.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-02-11T17:45:25.827Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6knr855zx5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6knr855zx5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Emmanuel","last_name":"Akabuogu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Victor ","last_name":"Martorelli","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rok","last_name":"Krašovec","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ian","last_name":"Roberts","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thomas","last_name":"Waigh","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cellular Imaging"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"t8ss7t539f","title":"Comparison of Bacterial Intracellular and Secreted Proteins produced in Milk Versus Medium for Escherichia coli by Proteomic Analysis","description":"Escherichia coli, Quantitative proteomics, Bacterial intracellular proteins, Secreted proteins","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-11-25T18:50:19.517Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/t8ss7t539f","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/t8ss7t539f"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"QIYUE","last_name":"SUN","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/027s68j25","name":"Ministry of Science and Technology of the People's Republic of China","grant_id":"2022YFD1301003"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"ZR2022MC184"}],"categories":["Proteomics","Escherichia coli"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gzyb3333vw","title":"NEW TERPENOIDS FROM Rytigynia kigeziensis Verdc AND THEIR ANTIBACTERIAL POTENTIAL ","description":"Spectroscopic data of compounds 1-15. 3DVisualization molecular docking results for compounds 7,13, 14, 15 and control against Staphylococcus aureus, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Klebsiella pneumoniae and Escherichia coli. 96-well plates for antibacterial screening","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-03-30T15:28:37.291Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gzyb3333vw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gzyb3333vw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"peter","last_name":"sekandi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jane","last_name":"Namukobe","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Robert","last_name":"Byamukama","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christine","last_name":"Nagawa","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jennifer","last_name":"Nambooze","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Markus","last_name":"Bacher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rosenau","last_name":"Thomas","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Linda","last_name":"Langat","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wilhelm","last_name":"Anke","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dulcie","last_name":"Mulholland","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/03dmz0111","name":"Makerere University","grant_id":"Makerere Research and Innovation Fund (Tract 5)"},{"id":"https://ror.org/02rqftv84","name":"Organisation for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons","grant_id":"L/ICA/ICB-368/23"}],"categories":["Phytochemical Isolation","Antibacterial Activity","Triterpenoid","Antioxidant Study","Sun Protection"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":131857,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":162,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":131857,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":131309,"restricted":66,"embargoed":482},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2160},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1591},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":623},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":455},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":437},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal d